      "question": "Pull the latest oil sample for Transformer 1 at site MAIN and list which dissolved gases (hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene, co) exceed the IEEE C57.104 Condition 1 single-gas limits. Also report the sample timestamp and how old the reading is. Only count combustible gases, and leave out co2, oxygen and nitrogen. If acetylene is missing from the newest record, use the most recent non-null acetylene value, give its timestamp separately, and say that it comes from an older sample.",
      "answer": "**Latest oil sample \u2014 Transformer 1 (site MAIN)**\n- Sample timestamp: **2024-01-19T00:00:00** (age \u2248 85,183,536 s \u2248 **2.7 years old**)\n- Acetylene present in the newest record (7 ppm), so no older sample was needed.\n\n**Dissolved gases vs IEEE C57.104 Condition 1 limits (combustible gases only):**\n\n| Gas | Value (ppm) | Condition 1 limit | Exceeds? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Hydrogen (H\u2082) | 2,850 | 100 | \u2705 Yes |\n| Methane (CH\u2084) | 7,400 | 120 | \u2705 Yes |\n| Ethane (C\u2082H\u2086) | 5,470 | 65 | \u2705 Yes |\n| Ethylene (C\u2082H\u2084) | 16,650 | 50 | \u2705 Yes |\n| Acetylene (C\u2082H\u2082) | 7 | 1 | \u2705 Yes |\n| Carbon monoxide (CO) | 32 | 350 | \u274c No |\n\n**Gases exceeding Condition 1 limits:** hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, and acetylene \u2014 all dramatically above their limits (e.g., ethylene is >300\u00d7 its limit). CO is well below its limit. CO\u2082, oxygen, and nitrogen excluded per your instructions.\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f Note: this pattern of severe exceedances warrants immediate DGA review \u2014 happy to run a Rogers Ratio interpretation if you'd like.",
